// =============================================================================
// daily-spend.dogwood — Dogwood temporal policy: per-policy-session spend fence
// =============================================================================
// Accompanies Chapter 13 (Section 13.12) of "AgentCore in Production: The
// Operator's Playbook", 2nd edition (October 2026).
//
// Dogwood is the open-source, Cedar-compatible language AgentCore Policy has
// accepted since August 6, 2026. Every valid Cedar policy is valid Dogwood;
// `when temporal { ... }` blocks add conditions over the history of the
// *policy session*. Syntax follows the AWS ML blog "Securing AI agents with
// temporal policies in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore" (Policy 4, cumulative cap).
//
// What this bounds: the sum of `amount_cents` on payments___pay_endpoint
// requests in the same policy session over the last 24 hours (the maximum
// window), including the current request. Above $40.00 the call is denied.
//
// What decides whether it bounds anything useful: the policy session id,
// sent in the x-amzn-bedrock-agentcore-policy-session-id header and chosen
// by YOUR backend, never by the agent. Derive it from (agent, tenant, UTC
// date), e.g. HMAC(key, "credit-analysis|acme-lending|2026-06-03"), so all of
// a day's application sessions share one history. One id per application
// session reproduces the Section 13.1 incident.
//
// Caveats from the author's October 2026 lab (5,294 calls):
//   * denied requests also count toward ::request limits (40 of 40 trials);
//   * an agent that rotates its own session id bypasses limits (0 of 20 blocked);
//   * creating, updating, or deleting ANY temporal policy invalidates every
//     live policy session; the next call returns HTTP 200 with JSON-RPC
//     -32005 and a new session starts with an empty history, so the day's
//     running total resets at every temporal-policy deploy.
// Quotas: 20 temporal policies per engine, 3 temporal operators per policy,
// 24 h maximum window.
//
// The tool's input schema must declare amount_cents as an integer (Long).
// cedarpy cannot evaluate this file; test it in LOG_ONLY against the Gateway.
// Syntax kept consistent with chapter-03/policies/temporal/.
// Local testing: cedarpy cannot parse Dogwood; validate on AWS only.
// Live-tested 2026-11-12 (cap set to 1000 cents, IAM principal in the lab):
//   * the sum includes the current request: 600 and 300 passed, 200 was denied;
//   * with `principal` left open the policy fails strict validation
//     ("Overly Restrictive"), so it is scoped to OAuth users;
//   * it does not count payments___purchase_access. Dogwood rejected both a
//     disjunction of two actions inside `formerly` and `a + b > cap` over two
//     sums, so daily-purchase.dogwood caps purchase_access on its own; the
//     combined ceiling is budget_guard.py's job.
// =============================================================================

forbid (
  principal is AgentCore::OAuthUser,
  action == AgentCore::Action::"payments___pay_endpoint",
  resource == AgentCore::Gateway::"arn:aws:bedrock-agentcore:us-east-1:111122223333:gateway/acme-lending"
)
when temporal {
  exists (total: Long). (
    (sum c for (c: Long), (t: Timepoint). where (
      formerly within 24h (
        AgentCore::Action::"payments___pay_endpoint"::request{
          input.amount_cents: c, eventResource: resource
        }
        && tp(t)
      )
    )) == total && total > 4000
  )
};
